# Automatic Nomor RM and required patient fields (rule 244)

Source: the internal memo of 2026-10-17 (M4.1, M4.2) and the owner's written decisions of 2026-11-17, recorded in
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1.md` §2c; the plan, impact analysis and compatibility plan are in
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1-memo-implementation-plan.md`. Delivered in PRs; each PR adds its section.

## 0. Owner decisions (CG-22)

- M4.1 "Auto, read-only": the system generates the Nomor RM; Front Office cannot edit it; the DaengtisiaMS format
  `DG-{KODE_CABANG}-{TAHUN}-{NOMOR}` is kept; locking and database uniqueness; existing numbers never changed; Legacy
  RME compatibility kept; CC1-PAT-1 measured read-only on production before the automatic number is switched on; safe
  overflow handling; the Sprint 23.8 manual-number rule revised explicitly (this rule).
- Plan defaults accepted (2026-11-17): (1) an old paper-card number only through Supervisor RME, with a reason,
  audited; (2) the no-phone exception through Front Office with a reason code, audited and reportable; (3) after
  unusually high existing numbers the sequence continues after the highest — a number is never reused; (4) the legacy
  patient CSV import commit needs the paper-card permission (staging stays with Front Office; revises part of rule 168);
  (5) no correction tool for existing numbers in this sprint — number, branch and registration date are frozen on edit.

## 1. The counter and the allocator (PR-1)

1. **The counter.** `mst_patient_rm_sequences` holds one row per branch and year (`UNIQUE(branch_id, year)`):
   `last_number` is the highest sequence issued or seeded. Additive migration only (CG-09).
2. **The allocator.** `PatientRmNumberAllocator::allocate(Branch, int $year)` is the only issuer:
   - it refuses to run outside the caller's open transaction (the registration transaction), so the row lock lasts
     until the patient row commits;
   - it refuses MAIN (never an RME branch) and an unknown branch code;
   - it locks the branch-year row FOR UPDATE BEFORE reading the counter (`lockOrCreate`: a missing row is created with
     INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, seeded from the highest issued number, then locked), so two registrations in one
     branch-year serialise and the second reads the first's committed value;
   - it skips a candidate while it exists in ANY spelling (`PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::numericSpellings`: the
     canonical code and its historical aliases from `BranchCodeAlias` — TKM1/TLK1, SUN4/SPN4 — times every zero padding
     up to nine digits) on any patient, soft-deleted ones included (rule 169: they reserve their number), or as an
     active merge alias (`PatientRmAliasRepositoryInterface::findActiveByNumbers`);
   - it composes with four-digit padding (`formatSequence`), widens past 9999 (10000, …) rather than stopping
     registration, logs a capacity warning from 9000 with branch id, year and digit count only (never a number), and
     refuses once a sequence would need nine digits (`PatientRmNumberUnavailableException`, an operator message
     without a number);
   - before the counter moves, the composed number must round-trip through `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::parse()`
     to the same branch code and sequence; a branch code the format cannot carry (a '-' in it, more than 16 characters)
     is refused with the same exception, never issued as an unparseable number.
3. **Never rewritten.** Nothing here updates `mst_patients`. `compose()` and `parse()` are unchanged; numbers issued
   before this rule, in any shape, stay as they are.
4. **Seeding.** `php artisan patient:rm-sequence-seed` (runtime user; refuses root, and fails closed — refuses — when
   the effective user cannot be determined, e.g. no posix extension): dry run by default — per branch-year counts
   (numbers, non-numeric sequences, above 9999, ignored as oversized, ignored above `--ignore-above`) and the proposed
   counter's DIGIT COUNT only (`PROPOSED_COUNTER_DIGITS`), totals for MAIN numbers, unknown codes and non-canonical
   values; never a Nomor RM, never the counter value (printed beside the branch and year it would complete a real
   patient's number; the repository is public, CG-11), never a patient detail. An existing sequence wider than the
   allocator issues (more than `PatientRmNumberAllocator::MAX_DIGITS` digits, e.g. a phone number typed as the
   sequence) can never collide with an issuable number, so the lazy seed and the plan both ignore it (counted as
   `IGNORED_OVERSIZED`): seeded, it would push the counter past the cap and, since a counter is never lowered, stop
   automatic numbering for that branch-year for good. `--apply` raises each counter
   to GREATEST(current, proposed) — never lower, idempotent — and writes one `PATIENT_RM_SEQUENCE_SEEDED` audit row of
   counts. Scope: the current clinic year (`ClinicalClock`) unless `--all-years`; `--ignore-above=N` keeps unusually
   high numbers out of the counter (they stay reserved: the allocator still skips them). Running `--apply` on
   production is a separate owner-approved step. The allocator seeds a missing branch-year on first use itself, so
   correctness never depends on the command.
5. **Lock order.** Counter row → patient insert → (later PRs) the visit's own locks. Since PR-2 the counter row is
   locked only through `PatientRmNumberAllocator`: `allocate()` (registration), `claimExisting()` (a typed number:
   the legacy CSV commit, PR-3's paper card and pilot importer) and `lockCounter()` (the sorted pre-lock of the CSV
   commit and of the pilot importer, §2.7);
   the seed command raises it with `raiseTo()`. No other code locks or writes it.
6. **Dormant (PR-1 only).** In PR-1 no registration path called the allocator. PR-2 switched NEW registrations to it
   (§2); a typed number survives only for a number that already exists outside the system (§2.6).

   **PR-2 must** (done — §2.5, §2.6) close the other Nomor RM writers (recorded by the PR-1 independent review, not implemented in PR-1):
   the legacy patient CSV import validation (`LegacyPatientImportService::takenValues`) and commit (its
   `lockForUpdate()->exists()` check), the explicit-number path in `PatientService` (an explicit `medical_record_number`
   kept verbatim) and `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::exists()` each check only the exact spelling and never take
   the branch-year counter lock. PR-2 routes every one of them through `numericSpellings` (every alias code and zero
   padding) plus the active merge aliases, under the branch-year counter lock (lock order of item 5), and compares
   case-insensitively (as `findActiveByNumbers` already does), so a manual or imported number can never take, or
   duplicate in another spelling, a number the allocator issues or will issue.
7. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/FrontOfficePatientRmNumberAllocatorTest.php` (AL-01..AL-15) and
   `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmSequenceSeedCommandTest.php` (SD-01..SD-10) — token `FrontOffice`;
   `tests/Feature/RME/ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrencyPatientRmSequenceTest.php` (PostgreSQL; committed writer + NOWAIT
   probe + `lock_timeout`; it pins that the counter row is locked when it is READ, not only when it is written) — token
   `ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrency`. All three are declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 2. The automatic number at registration (PR-2, active)

1. **One issuer for new patients.** `PatientService::create()` is the only path that registers a patient (Master Data →
   Tambah Pasien and Kunjungan → Pasien Baru through `ClinicVisitService`). Inside its transaction it resolves the
   branch (required — `branch_id` on the master form, the visit's branch on Pasien Baru; MAIN, an inactive or a
   non-RME branch is refused), stamps `registered_at` with `ClinicalClock::today()` and takes the number from
   `PatientRmNumberAllocator::allocate($branch, clinical year)`. A posted `medical_record_number`, `manual_rm_number`
   or `registered_at` is dropped by the service (and no longer validated by `StorePatientRequest` or
   `StoreClinicVisitRequest`); the request-time "Nomor RM final … sudah digunakan" pre-checks are gone because there is
   nothing to pre-check. 00:30 WITA on 1 January belongs to the new year although UTC is still 31 December.
   **Branch scope (PR-2 review F2, CG-05; CC1-PAT-2 store path).** The branch must be one the actor is working
   (`RmeWorkingBranchScope::allows()`): a context-bound desk account (Front Office, Admin Klinik, Kasir, Perawat) may
   register only into its working branch — the request may narrow, never widen. Master Data: the form offers only
   the allowed branches, `StorePatientRequest` answers **422** (`branch_id.in`) for another one (a stale or edited
   form is a user-correctable input error), and `PatientService::create($data, $actor)` answers **403** to any caller
   that still names one (an authorization breach, as `ClinicVisitService::resolveBranchId()` does). Kunjungan → Pasien
   Baru: `StoreClinicVisitRequest` pins the pinned desk account's new patient to its working branch and
   `ClinicVisitService::resolveBranchId()` re-asserts the scope (403); it passes no actor to `create()`, whose null
   actor means "every active RME branch" (passing it would add `ClinicVisitService` to this PR — recorded).
   **Every writer is governed** — the pilot backup importer (`rme:import-pilot-backup`) since PR-3 (§4.5).
2. **Retired.** The free override (a full `medical_record_number` typed on the master form) and the UTC fallback
   generator (`PatientCodeGenerator`, a CG-12 deviation; deleted with `PatientCodeGenerationTest`, and the
   `config/patient.php` block emptied) are gone. Existing rows
   keep whatever number they hold.
3. **Frozen on edit (default 5).** `PatientService::update()` drops `medical_record_number`, `manual_rm_number`,
   `branch_id` and `registered_at`; `UpdatePatientRequest` no longer accepts them; the edit form shows them read-only.
   There is no correction tool for existing numbers.
4. **Unique violations.** The insert runs in a savepoint inside the registration transaction, so the counter row lock
   taken by the allocator stays held across retries. A unique violation on `medical_record_number` (a writer that did
   not take the counter lock, e.g. an old PHP process during a rolling deploy) rolls back the insert only, the
   allocator issues the next number (the collided one is genuinely taken), and this is retried up to
   `PatientService::UNIQUE_RETRIES` (3) times — one attempt plus three retries — then a 422 on `medical_record_number`
   and the whole transaction rolls back (counter included). A `ktp_number` violation answers 422 on `ktp_number` at
   once. Any other unique violation is rethrown untouched (review F4). No raw SQL or number in a message.
   `PatientRmNumberUnavailableException` (MAIN, unknown or unparseable code, the 8-digit cap) answers 422 on
   `medical_record_number` with its operator message.
5. **One uniqueness question, in every spelling.** `PatientRmNumberAllocator::isTaken()` is the single answer to "is this
   Nomor RM taken?": `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::collisionSpellings()` (the value as given plus, once
   uppercased, every equivalent branch code × every zero padding up to 18 digits for a numeric sequence, or every
   equivalent code for a non-numeric one) checked against every patient, soft-deleted ones included, and every active
   merge alias. **Patients are compared by numeric VALUE in any padding (review F1):**
   `PatientRmSequenceRepository::anyNumberIssued()` matches a canonical all-digit number by its lowered prefix
   (`dg-{code}-{year}-`, every equivalent code) and `LTRIM(SUBSTR(...), '0')` = the value, so `…-000000000006` and a
   30-digit padding are the same number as `…-6`; `numericValueOf()` / `collisionKey()` apply the same rule in PHP
   (`takenAmong()`, the allocator's skip), so all three agree. Merge aliases are compared exactly against the spellings
   (an alias is a copy of its source patient's number, which the value comparison already covers). `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::exists()` delegates to it; `collisionKey()` gives one comparison
   key per identity (in-file duplicates in the legacy CSV); `takenAmong()` answers a whole batch in a bounded number of
   queries (one read per branch-year, one alias query per chunk of 50, one more per alias found).
6. **Numbers that already exist (legacy compatibility).** A number that comes from outside the system — the legacy
   patient CSV import now, the Supervisor RME paper-card exception and the pilot backup importer in PR-3 — is stored
   verbatim, never renumbered, and only after `PatientRmNumberAllocator::claimExisting($branch, $number)` has locked
   that number's branch-year counter row (lazily creating it, seeded like the allocator) and found no spelling taken.
   **A successful claim raises the counter** to the claimed value — GREATEST, never lower, under the same lock — so the
   sequence continues after the highest number on record (owner default 3, followed literally), within the claim cap
   below. The allocator's skip stays as the safety net and finds the first free value with ONE set-based read of the
   branch-year (`firstFreeAbove()`), not a per-number loop. `LegacyPatientImportService` refuses at staging a manual
   sequence longer than 18 digits, all zeros (review L1: `numericValueOf()` has no value for zero, so `…-0` and
   `…-00` would be two spellings no comparison joins), with a value wider than 8 digits (format capacity), or wider
   than 9 characters whose value fits 8 digits once leading zeros are stripped (a second spelling of an issuable
   number); stages and revalidates through `isTaken()`/`takenAmong()` and value-aware in-file keys; and its commit
   locks every branch-year counter the batch touches, sorted by branch then year, before the first insert, then
   claims each row with `claimExisting()` (review F6: one code path; it sees the batch's own earlier inserts and
   raises the counter). Archive branch derivation and alias folding are unchanged.

   **The claim cap (owner decisions 2026-11-17, "RM CLAIM CAP: refuse jump > +1000" and "Per batch"; closes review
   M1).** `claimExisting()` refuses — a `PatientRmNumberUnavailableException` with code
   `PatientRmNumberAllocator::CLAIM_REFUSED` (`isClaimRefusal()`), an Indonesian operator message without any number
   that tells the operator to escalate the case to Supervisor RME; nothing is accepted automatically and no counter
   moves:
   - **before the counter is locked** (`formatRefusal()`, which reads no counter): a year after the clinical year,
     whatever the value (review A4: never plausible; `CLAIM_FUTURE_YEAR_MESSAGE`); for the current clinical year a
     sequence that is not all digits (review A3); in any year an all-digit sequence whose value is zero or wider than
     `MAX_DIGITS` (8) digits (`CLAIM_CAPACITY_MESSAGE`). This replaces PR-2's "a wider value is only reserved": such a
     number is no longer accepted at all (existing rows keep whatever they hold);
   - **current clinical year (`ClinicalClock`), under the counter row lock** (the unique index as backstop): a value
     above `CLAIM_MAX_CURRENT_YEAR_VALUE` (9999), or more than `CLAIM_MAX_JUMP` (+1000) above the reference
     (`CLAIM_JUMP_MESSAGE`). The reference is (review A2) the locked counter, including this transaction's earlier
     claims, for a paper card (per claim: two cards `1010` then `2010` after a counter of 10 both pass); and for a
     batch (owner decision "Per batch") the BASELINE — the counter `lockCounter()` returned when the legacy CSV commit
     or the pilot backup run pre-locked that branch-year — so one batch or run raises a branch-year by at most +1000 in
     total (rows `1000`, `2000` after a counter of 10: the second is refused). A value at or below the counter moves
     nothing and is not a jump. Automatic numbering is unaffected (it still widens past 9999);
   - **a past year** only ever raises that year's counter (an 8-digit value is within capacity); it never moves the
     current year's, and a past-year sequence that is not all digits is only checked for "taken".
   A number is still never reused: a refused claim frees nothing, and the skip and "taken" checks are unchanged.
   Callers: the paper card answers 422 on `paper_card_sequence`; the CSV commit refuses the whole batch naming the row
   (`Baris N: …`), nothing committed; the pilot importer SKIPS the row (its per-row semantics; later rows within the
   run's +1000 still import, §4.5). CSV staging refuses a Timestamp in a year that has not started (review A4). Staging
   and the pilot dry run read no counter, so they do not predict a jump refusal; both refuse the format (the pilot
   importer on every path, a code with no branch included). The seed command's `raiseTo()` is not a claim and is not
   capped (an owner-approved step, §1.4). Not built (review A5, A6, optional): a seed-plan warning for a large
   counter jump and an advisory jump warning at CSV staging (both would need a counter read outside the allocator).
7. **Lock order and namespace.** Counter row (`mst_patient_rm_sequences`, `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`) → patient insert →
   the visit's own locks (`lockVisitSequence`, advisory 92_001, and later ones). The counter is a ROW lock, not an
   advisory lock, so it takes no number in the 91_xxx/92_xxx advisory inventory on
   `ClinicVisitRepository::PATIENT_ENCOUNTER_LOCK_NAMESPACE`. Every writer that locks MORE than one counter takes them
   all up front, sorted by branch id then year, before its first patient insert: the legacy commit (after its batch
   header and staging-row locks) and, since PR-3 review L3, the pilot backup importer (`lockRmCounters()`, before its
   patient loop). A registration or a paper card holds one counter. So no two writers ever wait on each other's
   counter rows in opposite orders (no lock-order cycle on counters); the pilot importer runs as ONE long transaction
   and holds its counters until it commits, so it is run outside clinic hours. Expression indexes on
   `LOWER(medical_record_number) text_pattern_ops` (mst_patients) and `LOWER(alias_medical_record_number)` serve the
   case-insensitive comparisons (additive migration `2026_11_18_100001`, PostgreSQL only, CONCURRENTLY).
8. **Views.** Both forms show "Nomor RM: otomatis — DG-{KODE}-{TAHUN}-####" with the clinical year (and the selected
   branch code once chosen); no number is previewed, because two operators would see the same one. The master form's
   success message carries the issued number; Pasien Baru shows it on the visit.
9. **Deploy.** `patient:rm-sequence-seed --apply` is NOT a prerequisite: the allocator seeds a missing branch-year from
   the highest number already issued (any spelling) on first use, under the same lock. Running the seed first only
   makes the counters visible in advance and lets `--ignore-above` keep an unusually high number out of a counter.
   Deploy outside clinic hours: a request served by a pre-deploy PHP process composes typed numbers without the lock
   (item 4 covers the collision). The additive index migration (item 7) runs with the deploy's
   `php artisan migrate --force`. Rollback is a code revert; the counter table and the indexes become inert.
10. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmAutoNumberTest.php` (AN-01..AN-26) — token `FrontOffice`;
    `tests/Feature/Patient/LegacyPatientRmAutoNumberCompatibilityTest.php` (LP-01..LP-13) — token `LegacyPatient`;
    `tests/Feature/RME/ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrencyPatientRmSequenceTest.php` (PostgreSQL, two connections in one
    process: the full registration, the import claim and the REAL `LegacyPatientImportService::commit()` queuing behind
    an in-flight registration) — token `ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrency`. AN-22..AN-26 and LP-09..LP-13 cover the
    review fixes. All declared in
    `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 3. Sprint 23.8 revised explicitly

Sprint 23 Phase 23.8 (`docs/sprint_23_phase_23_8_patient_id_registration_flow.md`, the
`PatientMedicalRecordNumberService` docblock) said: the RM sequence is typed by the admin, "NOT auto-generated and NOT
auto-padded", and the composed value is checked for uniqueness by exact match. Rule 244 revises it:

- **Kept:** the format `DG-{KODE_CABANG}-{TAHUN}-{NOMOR}`, `compose()`/`parse()`, global uniqueness (now in every
  spelling), the clinical calendar owning the year (rule 110 §4), and never renumbering an existing patient.
- **Replaced for new registrations:** the sequence is issued by the allocator, padded to four digits, widened past
  9999; the registration date is the server's clinical day; the admin types neither.
- **Narrowed:** a typed sequence survives only for a number that already exists outside the system (legacy CSV import;
  PR-3 paper card; pilot backup import), claimed under the counter lock and within the claim cap (§2.6).
- Rule 110 §4's list of request-time pre-check sites (`StorePatientRequest`, `UpdatePatientRequest`,
  `StoreClinicVisitRequest`) no longer applies: those pre-checks were removed with the typed number.

## 4. The paper-card exception and the legacy CSV commit (PR-3)

1. **One permission for every number a person enters.** `register_patient_paper_card_rm`
   (`PatientService::PAPER_CARD_PERMISSION`), together with `manage patients`, is the only permission under which a
   person may enter a Nomor RM (Master Data paper card, legacy CSV commit). The pilot backup importer is a CLI command
   with no actor or permission; it is gated by the claim alone (item 5). `PermissionSeeder`
   declares it; `RoleSeeder` grants it to **Supervisor RME** only (Super Admin through `'*'`; not Admin Klinik, so the
   Front Office = Admin Klinik + Kasir union is unchanged); it is classified under Master Data in
   `PermissionGroupingService`. Everyone else always receives the automatic number (§2).
2. **Paper card at registration (default 1).** Master Data → Tambah Pasien only. Kunjungan → Pasien Baru stays
   automatic for every role; a paper-card patient is registered in Master Data and then selected as an existing
   patient. The fields `paper_card_year`, `paper_card_sequence` and `paper_card_reason_code` (all or none) and the
   optional `paper_card_reason_note` (PR-3b, item 3):
   - are rendered only under `@can('register_patient_paper_card_rm')` (rule 135 mirror);
   - make `StorePatientRequest::authorize()` answer 403 to anyone without the permission (a posted value is refused,
     never silently honoured); the controller then asks `PatientPolicy::registerWithPaperCardNumber`, and
     `PatientService::createWithPaperCardNumber()` re-asserts the permission and `manage patients` (CG-06);
   - bounds, enforced in the service so they bind **Super Admin** too (Gate::before only answers the permission
     question): a configured reason code, a note of 10–500 characters after trim when given (required for
     `LAINNYA`); year 1900..clinical year; sequence 1–8 digits, not zero;
   - the branch must be inside the actor's working-branch scope, exactly as on the automatic path (§2.1).
   The number is `DG-{branch canonical code}-{year}-{sequence as printed}` (leading zeros kept). It is stored only
   after `PatientRmNumberAllocator::claimExisting()` locked that branch-year's counter and found no spelling taken
   (alias codes, padding, case, soft-deleted patients, active merge aliases) — otherwise 422 on `paper_card_sequence`,
   with no number in the message — and passed the claim cap (§2.6; 422 on `paper_card_sequence`, escalate to
   Supervisor RME). MAIN, an unknown code and a code the format cannot carry are 422; nothing is written. Only a
   `medical_record_number` unique violation on the insert is "taken"; a `ktp_number` one is 422 on `ktp_number`; any
   other unique violation is rethrown untouched (review I3, as §2.4). Like every claim (§2.6), a successful one raises the branch-year counter to the claimed value when it has
   at most 8 digits (continue after the highest). `manual_rm_number` keeps the
   printed sequence; `registered_at` is the clinical day of entry (the card's own date is never invented).
3. **The reason: stored, restricted, immutable (PR-3b; owner decision 2026-11-17, "PAPER-CARD REASON: store text,
   restricted").** Revises PR-3's "required at entry but not stored".
   - **Entry.** `paper_card_reason_code` is one of config `patient.paper_card_reasons` (code => Indonesian label:
     `KARTU_LAMA_PASIEN_LAMA` "Pasien lama membawa kartu berobat kertas", `MIGRASI_ARSIP_KERTAS` "Migrasi arsip rekam
     medis kertas", `LAINNYA` "Lainnya (wajib catatan)"; a used code is never renamed or removed, new ones are added);
     `paper_card_reason_note` is optional, 10–500 characters after trim, REQUIRED for `LAINNYA` (blank = none), valid
     UTF-8 without control characters (tab, line feed, carriage return allowed; `PAPER_CARD_NOTE_PATTERN`, review B1).
     Checked by `StorePatientRequest` and again in `createWithPaperCardNumber()` (binds Super Admin). Whoever may
     register the paper card (`register_patient_paper_card_rm` + `manage patients`) enters it; the form warns not to
     write identity data (NIK, name, address, phone) in the note.
   - **Storage.** `trx_patient_rm_paper_card_registrations` (additive migration `2026_11_18_100002`): `patient_id`,
     `branch_id`, `year` (the card's), `reason_code`, `reason_note` (≤500), `actor_user_id`, `created_at`; foreign keys
     `trx_prpcr_patient_fk`, `trx_prpcr_branch_fk`, `trx_prpcr_actor_fk` (all RESTRICT), indexes
     `trx_prpcr_patient_idx`, `trx_prpcr_branch_created_idx`. Written by
     `PatientRmPaperCardRegistrationRepositoryInterface::record()` inside the claim's transaction (it refuses to run
     outside one), so a refused claim (taken, claim cap, KTP) stores no reason. A database failure on that insert is
     rethrown as a `RuntimeException` with no value and no previous exception (review B1: the driver error carries the
     bindings, the note among them, and a reported exception reaches `laravel.log`); the request answers 500.
   - **Retention.** Immutable for the life of the patient record: no `updated_at`; the model
     (`PatientRmPaperCardRegistration`) throws on update and delete; the repository offers no update or delete; the
     foreign keys RESTRICT (patients and users are soft-deleted, so the row survives them); on PostgreSQL the
     `trx_prpcr_no_update_delete` and `trx_prpcr_no_truncate` triggers (function `trx_prpcr_refuse_change()`, same
     migration) refuse UPDATE, DELETE and TRUNCATE for every code path (review B5). A patient merge leaves the
     row on the patient it was registered for (`PatientOwnedRecordRegistry::PRESERVE`, like the RM alias source: that
     patient keeps the number). There is no change path today; any future correction MUST append a NEW row with its
     own audit row — never edit or delete a row.
   - **Reading.** Only `view_patient_rm_paper_card_reasons` (`PatientService::PAPER_CARD_REASON_READ_PERMISSION`):
     `PermissionSeeder` declares it, `RoleSeeder` grants it to **Supervisor RME** and **Owner** (read only; the Owner
     cannot enter a paper card); **Super Admin** reads through `'*'` and `Gate::before`, by design. Grouped under Master
     Data. Two routes OUTSIDE the `manage patients` group (the Owner has no `manage patients`), both behind the
     permission middleware and `cache.headers:private;no_store;max_age=0`: `settings.patients.paper-card-registrations.index`
     (the reader's branch scope, newest first, WITHOUT the note) and `settings.patients.paper-card-registrations.show`
     (`{patient}` = `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`; codes and notes). `PatientPolicy::viewAnyPaperCardRegistrations` /
     `viewPaperCardRegistrations`, then `PatientService::paperCardRegistrationList()` /
     `paperCardRegistrationsFor()` re-assert the permission (403) and the working-branch scope (CG-05,
     `RmeWorkingBranchScope`): a patient outside it is **404**, and only rows inside it are returned. The per-patient
     page binds a soft-deleted patient too (`withTrashed()`, review B3: the list links to it) and also lists, read only
     and labelled "Dari pasien yang digabungkan", the rows of patients merged INTO it (`merged_into_patient_id`, one
     level, same branch filter; review B4). Each read that returns rows writes a `PATIENT_RM_PAPER_CARD_REASON_VIEWED`
     audit row with `patient_id`, `actor_user_id`, `registration_ids` only (review B8). Linked from the sidebar and the
     patient edit page under `@can` only.
   - **Privacy.** The `PATIENT_RM_PAPER_CARD_REGISTERED` audit row (same transaction) carries exactly `patient_id`,
     `branch_id`, `actor_user_id`, `registration_id`, `reason_code` — never the note, the number, the year or the
     sequence. The note is never logged, never part of a general export (no export reads the table), and never
     flashed into the session on a refused submit (`paper_card_reason_note` is in `dontFlash`, bootstrap/app.php; the
     form does not refill it).
4. **Legacy patient CSV commit (default 4; revises part of rule 168).** Staging, review, revalidation and cancel stay
   with `manage patients`. The commit needs the paper-card permission on three layers: the route
   (`permission:register_patient_paper_card_rm`), `ConfirmLegacyPatientImportRequest::authorize()`, and
   `LegacyPatientImportService::commit()`, which refuses — before anything else, with an `AuthorizationException` and a
   `LEGACY_PATIENT_BATCH_CONFIRM_BLOCKED` audit row (`reason = paper_card_permission_missing`) — a committer id that is
   null, unknown, or lacks the paper-card permission or `manage patients` (review I6: the same pair the paper card
   re-asserts). A row past the claim cap refuses the whole batch, naming the row (§2.6). The preview shows the commit form only to holders (others see who confirms).
5. **Pilot backup importer.** `rme:import-pilot-backup` (`PilotBackupImportService`) is a CLI command run by an
   operator on the host: it has no actor and checks no permission, so the paper-card permission does not apply to it;
   it is gated by the claim alone. It no longer creates a patient whose number is held in another spelling: a number
   no live patient holds exactly is claimed with `claimExisting()` under the counter lock (live runs, which are
   transactional) or checked with `isTaken()` (dry run, or a code with no RME branch); a taken one, or one refused by
   the claim cap (§2.6), is skipped with a message that carries no number (the cap message sends the case to
   Supervisor RME). A live run first locks every counter its rows name, sorted (§2.7, review L3), and holds them until
   the import commits: run it outside clinic hours. The dry run reads no counter and does not predict a jump refusal
   (the format capacity is refused on every path).
   An exact live match is still updated as before (its number never changes).
6. **Remaining writers.** None that stores a typed number outside the gates above. `StressSeedPatientsCommand` writes
   synthetic `DG-{code}-2026-{8 digits}` rows directly and is refused outside local/stress/testing — a recorded residual,
   never production.
7. **Release.** PR-2, PR-3 and PR-3b ship in ONE release (plan compatibility item 3): PR-2 alone would leave a legacy
   CSV commit to every `manage patients` holder and no path for old cards, and PR-3 without PR-3b would take a reason
   the owner wants stored. After the deploy run `PermissionSeeder`, then `RoleSeeder`, then `permission:cache-reset`,
   as the runtime user (PR-3's `register_patient_paper_card_rm`, PR-3b's `view_patient_rm_paper_card_reasons`). PR-2's
   additive index migration (§2.7) and PR-3b's additive table migration (`2026_11_18_100002`, item 3) run with the
   deploy's `php artisan migrate --force`; PR-3 adds no migration. Rollback is a code revert; the table stays (never
   dropped on production).
8. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmPaperCardExceptionTest.php` (PC-01..PC-20; PC-18..PC-20 are
   review I3, I6 and L3) and `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmClaimCapTest.php` (CC-01..CC-19, the claim
   cap) — token `FrontOffice`, both declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`; LP-10, LP-13 and LP-14 (format
   capacity, review L1) in the `LegacyPatient` suite; the two claim-cap races (PostgreSQL, two connections) in the
   `ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrency` suite; `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmPaperCardReasonTest.php`
   (PR-01..PR-28, PR-3b: entry, read/deny matrix, 404 scope, immutability, privacy; PR-21..PR-28 are review B1, B3,
   B4, B5, B8; token `FrontOffice`, declared);
   review I1 (the Master Data KTP capture branch resolved directly, patient
   branch ≠ working branch) in `PatientKtpArchiveBranchIsolationTest` (token `PatientKtp`). The legacy CSV suites
   commit through `legacyPatientCommitterId()` (tests/Pest.php), an account holding the permission and
   `manage patients`.

## 5. Required core fields and the no-phone exception (PR-4)

Owner decision 2026-11-17 (memo M4.2): "Core fields, NIK optional. A controlled exception for patients without a
phone. Existing patients are never blocked." Plan default 2 (accepted 2026-11-17): the no-phone exception belongs to
Front Office, with a reason, audited and reportable.

1. **One rule builder for both forms.** `App\Modules\Patient\Support\PatientCoreFields::rules()` is the only source of
   the core-field rules. `StorePatientRequest` (Master Data → Tambah Pasien) spreads it at the top level and
   `StoreClinicVisitRequest` (Kunjungan → Pasien Baru) under `new_patient.`; on an existing-patient visit the hidden
   panel's core fields are `exclude`d, never validated (as rule 184 §13 does for KTP state). Required for a NEW
   patient:
   - gender (`Male`, `Female`, `Other`);
   - place of birth — the new additive column `mst_patients.place_of_birth` (≤100, one line, no control character);
   - date of birth — from 1900-01-01 to the clinical day (`ClinicalClock`, CG-12: at 00:30 WITA the new day already
     counts while UTC is still on the previous one);
   - phone OR WhatsApp (`required_without_all` over the WhatsApp number and the no-phone reason code);
   - address (≤1000) and occupation (≤150).
   A missing or invalid field is a 422 on that field (`new_patient.<field>` on the visit form), in Indonesian
   (`PatientCoreFields::messages()`), and nothing is written: no patient, no visit, no Nomor RM. The forms mark the
   fields with `*` and use no native `required` (a hidden required control is refused silently by browsers, rule 184
   §13). `PatientService` does not re-validate the core fields themselves: they are data quality, enforced at the two
   forms; the exception below is authorization and is re-asserted there.
2. **NIK optional.** NIK and the KTP scan are never required (rule 184 §10 unchanged; the KTP keys stay declared in
   each request's own `rules()`, so its source scan still reads them). A NIK typed at a new registration is exactly 16
   digits (`PatientCoreFields::NIK_RULES`). KTP masking is unchanged (CG-11).
3. **The no-phone exception (plan default 2).**
   - **Permission.** `register_patient_without_phone` (`PatientCoreFields::WITHOUT_PHONE_PERMISSION`):
     `PermissionSeeder` declares it; `RoleSeeder` grants it to **Front Office** and to **Admin Klinik** (the
     registration half of Front Office, so the Front Office = Admin Klinik + Kasir union stays exact; production has
     no Admin Klinik account); Super Admin through `'*'`. Not Kasir, not Supervisor RME. Grouped under Master Data.
   - **Fields.** `no_phone_reason_code`, one of config `patient.no_phone_reasons` (`TIDAK_PUNYA_TELEPON`,
     `TANGGUNGAN_TANPA_TELEPON`, `LAINNYA`; a used code is never renamed or removed), and `no_phone_reason_note`
     (10–500 characters, required for `LAINNYA`; tab, line feed and carriage return are the only control characters
     allowed). Only with neither number (`prohibited` beside a phone or WhatsApp number). Rendered only under
     `@can('register_patient_without_phone')` on both forms (rule 135 mirror), disabled (so not sent) until the
     checkbox is ticked.
   - **Three layers (CG-06).** The route (`manage patients`; the visit route plus the `create` patient ability), the
     FormRequest (`authorize()` answers 403 to a non-holder sending any no-phone field, on both requests), and
     `PatientService::create()` / `createWithPaperCardNumber()`, which re-assert the permission and every bound for
     any caller (Super Admin included: `Gate::before` only answers the permission question). Kunjungan → Pasien Baru
     passes its registrar as `$exceptionActor`; the branch-scope actor stays null there (§2.1).
   - **Storage.** The OPEN exception lives on the patient row: `no_phone_reason_code` (≤40), `no_phone_reason_note`
     (≤500), `no_phone_recorded_at` (additive migration `2026_11_18_100003`, with `place_of_birth`). Only
     `PatientService` writes them: they are stripped from every create and update payload and set for an accepted
     exception alone. The note is `$hidden` on the model (never in `toArray()`/JSON: selectors, exports, snapshots),
     never in an audit payload or a log, and shown only on the patient's edit page while the exception is open.
   - **Audit.** `PATIENT_NO_PHONE_EXCEPTION_RECORDED` (`PatientService::AUDIT_NO_PHONE_RECORDED`), written in the
     registration transaction, carries exactly `patient_id`, `branch_id`, `actor_user_id`, `reason_code`.
   - **Report.** Open exceptions are `mst_patients.no_phone_reason_code IS NOT NULL`; the history is the audit rows of
     the two actions. This PR adds no report page.
4. **Existing patients are never blocked.** `UpdatePatientRequest` keeps every field optional (place of birth
   included). A stored date of birth or NIK is never re-judged: only a value the operator CHANGES is held to the
   new-patient format (1900..the clinical day; 16 digits), so a legacy record always saves. The production measurement
   (sprint record §10.2, 2026-11-17: of 1,522 live patients, 1 lacks a date of birth, 5 lack phone and WhatsApp, 2 lack
   an address, 103 lack an occupation; 84 NIK values are not 16 digits; none has a place of birth) is legacy data the
   rules never force. **Closing.** When a patient has a phone or WhatsApp number after an edit,
   `PatientRepositoryInterface::closeNoPhoneException()` reads the open exception FOR UPDATE in the edit's transaction
   and clears the three columns, and `PatientService::update()` writes `PATIENT_NO_PHONE_EXCEPTION_CLEARED`
   (`patient_id`, `branch_id`, `actor_user_id`, the closed `reason_code`) exactly once. An edit never opens an
   exception and never writes its columns.
5. **Rules revised.** Rule 184 §9 ("typed data entry with no photo at all is a complete registration"): a complete NEW
   registration now means the core fields above; never applied to an existing, legacy or repeat-visit patient and never
   a visit blocker for one; §10 (no KTP field ever required) kept. AIR-PAT-21 / rules 177 §5 and 179: the place of
   birth is now a stored field; the KTP OCR suggestion for it is PR-5 (JS only) and nothing here writes an OCR value.
6. **Not changed (recorded).** The "Audit Data Pasien" completeness report (`PatientDataCompletenessService`) does not
   score the place of birth: every existing patient has none, so all 1,522 would turn incomplete — a separate
   decision. A patient merge does not reconcile `place_of_birth` (`PatientMergeField`) and does not close the
   exception when the reconciled row gains a number; an edit closes it.
7. **Release.** The additive migration runs with the deploy's `php artisan migrate --force`; then `PermissionSeeder`,
   `RoleSeeder`, `permission:cache-reset` as the runtime user. Rollback is a code revert; the columns stay (never
   dropped on production). PR-4 may ship after the PR-2/PR-3/PR-3b release or with it.
8. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficeNewPatientCoreFieldsTest.php` (CF-01..CF-24), token `FrontOffice`,
   declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`. Other suites register patients through `newPatientCoreFields()`
   (tests/Pest.php).
